    {
      "short": "Idempotency",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "title": "Idempotency & CAP in production",
      "body": [
        "At-least-once delivery (retries, queues, failovers) means duplicate writes are a <em>certainty</em>, not a risk. <span class=\"term\">Idempotency keys</span> — a client-generated unique id checked before applying a write — make the duplicate a no-op. That's how payment systems sleep at night.",
        "<span class=\"term\">CAP theorem</span> stopped being theory: during the partition you chose either consistency (reject writes, stay correct) or availability (accept writes, reconcile later) — payments choose consistency; carts choose availability, <em>per-operation</em>, not per-system."
      ],
      "callout": {
        "kind": "insight",
        "text": "Idempotency requires a dedup store and discipline in every write path — boring, unglamorous, and absolutely mandatory."
      },
